    "duration_min": 11
   },
   "body": "# 7 Things That Instantly Fail Your MOT in 2026 (UK Drivers)\n# UK Driving Explained\n# https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X3f81opiQZg\n\n[00:00] One in three cars fails its MOT in the UK. But here's the frustrating part. Most of those failures are completely preventable. Simple things you could check yourself in 10 minutes at home. A blown bulb, low tire tread, a small crack in the windscreen. But people don't check. They turn up for the MOT, fail instantly, and then end up paying hundreds to fix something they could have spotted for free. In this video, I'm going to show you the\n[00:33] seven most common instant MOT failures in 2026, what causes them, how to spot them before the test, and how to avoid them completely. And some of these are so simple, you could fix them today in under 5 minutes. Nearly 20% of all MOT failures are lighting faults. That's one in five cars failing for something as simple as a light bulb. Blown headlights, faulty brake lights, indicators that don't flash, cracked\n[01:08] light housings, even number plate lights that don't work. All instant fails. Here's what the tester checks. Headlights, both dipped and full beam. Front and rear indicators, brake lights, rear lights, fog lights, number plate lights, and hazard lights. Everything has to work. Everything has to be the correct color, and there can't be cracks in the housing or moisture trapped inside. How to check this yourself.\n[01:42] Get someone to help and turn on every light one by one while they walk around the car. Headlights, indicators, brake lights, hazards. Check every single one. Look closely at the light housings, too. Any cracks? Any condensation inside? Both can fail an MOT. And check your number plate lights at night. If the plate isn't clearly visible, the bulb needs replacing. This check takes 5 minutes and costs pennies to fix.\n[02:16] But it's still the number one MOT failure every year. Don't be the person who fails for a £20 bulb. Suspension faults cause nearly 20% of MOT failures. The tricky part is you often can't see suspension problems just by looking. Things like worn shock absorbers, broken springs, leaking dampers, worn bushes, or corroded components are hidden underneath the car. And you might not notice them until the\n[02:49] car goes up on the MOT ramp. But there are warning signs. If your car bounces more than once when you push down on a corner, your shock absorbers could be worn. If you hear clunking over bumps, that can mean worn suspension bushes. If the car pulls to one side while driving, it could be suspension or alignment issues. And if the car looks lower on one side, that's often a broken spring. If you notice any of these signs before\n[03:21] your MOT, get it checked early because suspension repairs can get expensive quickly. A single shock absorber might cost 50 to 150 pounds. A full set can easily be 300 to 600 pounds. Catching it early means avoiding a surprise failure on MOT day. Around 10% of MOT failures are caused by brake problems, which is scary when you think about it. That means one in 10\n[03:55] cars on the road has brake issues serious enough to fail. Common failures include worn brake pads, worn discs, low brake fluid, corroded brake lines, faulty handbrakes, or uneven braking. Any of those can result in an instant fail. If your car has done over 50,000 miles and the brake pads or discs have never been changed, there's a good chance they're getting close to the limit. Electric car owners should pay attention\n[transcript continues in field-notes/staging/X3f81opiQZg.txt]",
   "score": 1.0
